@flexiarg writing-coherence/refuse-the-cut-and-dried-answer
@title Refuse the Cut-And-Dried Answer
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 2 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-BvxmAN turn-FGKYBD; first proposed for: A paragraph under joint editing jumps quickly from a hard question to a neat answer.
@why [relationship-coherence/narrative-suspension]

! conclusion: Object to the premature closure as 'cut and dried'/'ostentatious' and ask for wording that keeps the difficulty visible.

  + IF:
    The prose resolves a tension that the underlying work has not actually resolved.

  + HOWEVER:
    A quick answer reads well and is easy to write; holding the tension open reads as less finished and takes more words.

  + THEN:
    Object to the premature closure as 'cut and dried'/'ostentatious' and ask for wording that keeps the difficulty visible.

  + BECAUSE:
    Narrative suspension prefers held-open incoherence over a distorting order; this applies it to paragraph editing: the polished answer is the distorting order, the refusal is the suspension.
